  "knowledgePattern": "Track the circle centre through ordered transformations",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Find the centre and radius",
      "body": "<p>Complete the square in both variables to put the circle into centre-and-radius form.</p>",
      "recap": "The centre is (a,b), and the radius is r.",
      "pitfall": "The number on the right is the radius squared.",
      "pause": "Mark the centre on a sketch and label the radius.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l13",
          "reminder": "Completing both squares reveals the circle’s centre and radius."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the centre one step at a time",
      "body": "<p>Make a row for the centre after each stated transformation. Keep a second row for the radius.</p>",
      "recap": "Reflection in y=x swaps coordinates; reflection in the x-axis changes the y-coordinate’s sign.",
      "pitfall": "Use the transformations in the order given.",
      "pause": "Finish both rows, then form the new circle equation.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l05",
          "reminder": "An enlargement about the origin multiplies coordinates and lengths by its scale factor."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Complete the squares: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math>. The centre is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the radius is 4.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> The successive centre positions are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>21</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Reflection in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> swaps the coordinates, reflection in the x-axis changes the second sign, and enlargement about the origin multiplies both coordinates by 3.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Only the enlargement changes the radius, taking it to 12. Hence the final equation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>9</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>21</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>144</mn></mrow></math>. Answer A. The squared radius is multiplied by 9, not 3.</p>"
}
